<configuration name="xml_curl.conf" description="cURL XML Gateway">
  <bindings>
    <binding name="example">
      <!-- The url to a gateway cgi that can generate xml similar to
	   what's in this file only on-the-fly (leave it commented if you dont
	   need it) -->
      <!-- one or more |-delim of configuration|directory|dialplan -->
      <!-- <param name="gateway-url" value="http://www.freeswitch.org/gateway.xml" bindings="dialplan"/> -->
      <!-- set this to provide authentication credentials to the server -->
      <!--<param name="gateway-credentials" value="muser:mypass"/>-->
      <!-- set to true to disable Expect: 100-continue lighttpd requires this setting -->
      <!--<param name="disable-100-continue" value="true"/>-->

      <!-- optional: if enabled this will disable CA root certificate checks by libcurl -->
      <!-- note: default value is disabled. only enable if you want this! -->
      <!-- <param name="ignore-cacert-check" value="true" /> -->
      <!-- one or more of these imply you want to pick the exact variables that are transmitted -->
      <!--<param name="enable-post-var" value="Unique-ID"/>-->

      <!-- optional: for HTTP(S) gateway urls, decode a BadgerFish JSON provisioning response
           instead of XML. With value "json" the module sends Accept: application/json. A "file:"
           gateway url is read off disk before any request, so it is always XML.

           The accepted shape is a restricted profile: one JSON object whose single key names the
           requested section. Within an element, "@name" is a string attribute and "$" is string
           text; any other member is a child, as a nested object or a non empty array of objects.
           Numbers, booleans and nulls are refused; an element carries either "$" or children,
           never both; and the top level object carries no "@" attribute member of any name,
           since the module builds the document and section envelope itself.

           The profile is bounded as well, so a runaway or hostile response cannot exhaust memory.
           A document may nest 32 levels deep and may carry 50000 values in total; one object may
           carry 256 members, one array 256 elements, and one parent 256 children of any single
           name; the whole document may build 20000 elements and attributes; one string value may
           be 8192 bytes and one name 128 bytes. Exceeding any of these is refused like any other
           shape error. The existing response-max-bytes parameter still caps the response body
           itself, for JSON exactly as it does for XML.

           A response that is not application/json, or whose body falls outside that profile, is
           logged as a warning and parsed as XML instead. When omitted, responses are decoded as
           XML.

           The operator runbook at src/mod/xml_int/mod_xml_curl/README.response-format.md
           documents this contract in full: both fallback reasons and their log and event
           signatures, the eleven resource ceilings, the "file:" boundary, the TLS hardening
           parameters, cookie jar placement, and the mod_h323 / mod_opal co-load prohibition. -->
      <!-- <param name="response-format" value="json"/> -->
    </binding>
  </bindings>
</configuration>
